Thank you for the materials provided under NDA and for the detailed cooperation from your side during the initial due diligence phase.

Following our review of the documentation made available to date, we are pleased to confirm that the information received broadly supports the main points set out in your initial presentation regarding the QuickFund portfolio, borrower base, servicing environment, repayment infrastructure, financial history, operational processes and technical setup.

On that basis, ESTO remains interested in exploring a transaction and we believe it is appropriate to move from the preliminary review phase to a more transaction-focused discussion.

At this stage, our internal assessment is that the most efficient structure may be an acquisition of the portfolio together with the operational assets required to preserve continuity. This would include, subject to final agreement, the receivables portfolio, relevant borrower and loan data, repayment schedules, customer-facing interface for existing borrowers, administration panel, servicing workflows, payment and reconciliation logic, technical documentation, migration support and an API-based integration layer allowing the QuickFund environment to connect with ESTO’s internal systems.

Our preference would be to structure the transaction in a way that allows ESTO to capture the economic and operational value of the QuickFund activity while avoiding unnecessary complexity where possible. In practical terms, this could mean acquiring the full operating package necessary for continuity, without necessarily acquiring the corporate entity itself, unless a share acquisition proves to be more efficient from a legal, regulatory, contractual or operational perspective.

We therefore suggest keeping both routes open at this stage:

1. an asset transaction covering the receivables portfolio and the full operational package required for borrower continuity; and
2. a corporate transaction / share acquisition, if legal and regulatory analysis confirms that this route would materially improve continuity or reduce transfer complexity.

For clarity, we do not currently view the legal form of the transaction as the main value driver. The key points for ESTO are the enforceability and collectability of the receivables, continuity for existing borrowers, data and payment migration, compliance comfort, access to the servicing environment, and the ability to integrate the QuickFund workflow into ESTO’s internal operating model.

In this context, we would like to discuss a transaction perimeter based on the following principles:

* acquisition of the active consumer credit receivables portfolio;
* transfer of all loan-level data, repayment schedules, payment history and borrower records necessary for servicing and collection;
* maintenance of the existing borrower interface for current QuickFund customers during a defined transition or run-off period;
* discontinuation of new QuickFund origination after completion, unless otherwise agreed;
* redirection or repositioning of the public-facing QuickFund landing pages toward ESTO, while preserving access for existing borrowers;
* transfer or licence of the administration panel, servicing tools and technical components required to manage the portfolio;
* API integration between the QuickFund environment and ESTO’s internal systems;
* delivery of technical documentation, database schema, deployment documentation, payment flow documentation and reconciliation logic;
* transition support from QuickFund for a defined period;
* controlled migration of payment flows, borrower communications and operational responsibilities;
* appropriate representations, warranties and indemnities covering the portfolio, legal enforceability, consumer credit compliance, data protection, intellectual property, financial information and absence of undisclosed liabilities.

The proposed API integration is particularly important for us. Based on the discussions to date, we understand that QuickFund would be prepared to provide an integration layer allowing ESTO to access and synchronise relevant data and workflows from the QuickFund system. We would like this to be documented in more detail, including the expected endpoints, authentication method, data fields, event triggers, documentation, testing environment, delivery timeline, acceptance criteria and post-delivery support.

From an operational perspective, our preferred model would be to avoid any disruption for existing borrowers. Existing customers should continue to have access to their repayment information, contracts, messages and payment instructions through a stable interface, while ESTO gradually assumes operational control and integrates the relevant data into its own environment. We believe this approach is likely to protect repayment performance and reduce migration risk.

Regarding the QuickFund brand and public interface, we would expect that, following completion, QuickFund would no longer be marketed as a separate active origination platform. The public website could either be redirected to ESTO or repositioned in a way to be agreed between the parties, while the borrower login area remains available to existing customers during the transition or run-off period.

We would also like to discuss the appropriate legal treatment of the QuickFund entity itself. If the transaction is structured as an asset transaction, we understand that the seller may subsequently wind down or liquidate the entity at its discretion, subject to applicable legal, regulatory and customer protection requirements. If the transaction is structured as a share acquisition, ESTO would expect the company to be operated only for controlled servicing / run-off purposes unless otherwise agreed, with no new origination activity and with a clear post-closing simplification plan.

At this stage, we would propose the following next steps:

1. organise a management call to discuss transaction structure and preferred perimeter;
2. organise a technical call focused specifically on the API integration, admin panel, borrower portal, payment flows and migration roadmap;
3. request a short legal memo or joint counsel discussion comparing an asset transaction and a share acquisition from the perspective of continuity, regulatory treatment, data protection, borrower notification and execution complexity;
4. agree the preferred transaction path;
5. prepare a non-binding letter of intent setting out transaction perimeter, commercial principles, exclusivity, conditions, due diligence confirmatory items and expected timetable;
6. proceed to confirmatory due diligence and transaction documentation.

For the avoidance of doubt, any further step remains subject to internal approvals, satisfactory confirmatory due diligence, final legal and regulatory review, agreement on transaction documentation, and agreement on commercially acceptable terms. Nothing in this email should be interpreted as a binding offer or commitment to proceed with a transaction.

That said, the materials reviewed to date are sufficiently encouraging for ESTO to continue discussions and work towards a structured proposal. We believe the opportunity may be attractive if the transaction is designed around operational continuity, disciplined integration and appropriate legal protections for both parties.

Please let us know your availability for a management call this week or early next week. We would suggest that the first call focus on transaction structure and integration perimeter, followed by a separate technical session with the relevant product, engineering and operations contacts.

Kind regards,

Dear,

Thank you for your detailed message and for the constructive feedback following ESTO’s initial due diligence review.

We are pleased to hear that the documentation provided to date broadly supports the main points set out in our initial presentation regarding the QuickFund portfolio, borrower base, servicing environment, repayment infrastructure, financial history, operational processes and technical setup.

We also appreciate ESTO’s continued interest in moving towards a more transaction-focused discussion.

From QuickFund’s perspective, we are aligned with the general direction outlined in your email. We agree that the most efficient structure may be an acquisition of the active consumer credit receivables portfolio together with the operational assets required to preserve borrower continuity, servicing continuity and repayment performance.

We also agree that both potential structures should remain open at this stage:

1. an asset transaction covering the receivables portfolio and the full operational package required for continuity; and
2. a corporate transaction / share acquisition, if legal and regulatory analysis confirms that this route materially improves continuity, transferability, data migration, borrower notification, regulatory treatment or execution certainty.

Our current preference would be to avoid unnecessary complexity where possible. However, we remain open to the structure that best preserves value, execution certainty and operational continuity for both parties.

Transaction perimeter

The transaction perimeter currently being discussed should, in our view, be assessed as a full operating package rather than as a receivables-only sale.

The package would include:

* the active consumer credit receivables portfolio;
* borrower and loan-level data necessary for servicing and collection;
* repayment schedules;
* historical payment records;
* borrower records required for continuity;
* existing borrower interface during a defined transition or run-off period;
* administration panel;
* servicing workflows;
* payment tracking and reconciliation logic;
* Stripe and bank transfer payment flow documentation;
* technical documentation;
* database schema;
* deployment documentation;
* migration support;
* borrower communication support;
* source code transfer, subject to final transaction documentation and completion;
* defined API / integration layer;
* and transition support from QuickFund and the group’s technical resources, including VM Cloud where relevant.

The key value proposition is that ESTO would not be acquiring only receivables. ESTO would be acquiring an operating credit servicing environment with existing borrower continuity, repayment infrastructure, servicing tools, data history and technical assets required to manage the portfolio without unnecessary disruption.

Commercial basis

Given the full perimeter now being discussed, QuickFund’s commercial position is that the transaction should be discussed on the basis of a EUR 700,000 valuation for the full operating package.

This valuation reflects the proposed transfer of the portfolio together with the borrower continuity layer, administration tools, servicing environment, payment logic, data structure, technical documentation, source code, migration support and a reasonable integration layer.

If ESTO wishes to discuss a materially different valuation, the transaction perimeter would need to be adjusted accordingly. In particular, any lower valuation would need to be reflected in the scope of:

* source code transfer;
* software rights;
* API / integration work;
* duration and scope of transition support;
* post-completion technical assistance;
* public website and borrower portal treatment;
* domain and brand usage during run-off;
* and the level of involvement expected from QuickFund and VM Cloud.

API and integration layer

We understand that the API integration layer is particularly important for ESTO.

QuickFund is prepared to support a structured integration process. The current system already includes the core elements required for integration, including borrower and loan data structures, repayment tracking, payment status logic, exports, webhook-based event handling and token-based access logic.

The remaining work would mainly consist of documenting and packaging a clean integration layer tailored to ESTO’s internal requirements, subject to a clearly agreed scope.

We suggest that the technical session covers:

* expected endpoints;
* authentication method;
* access control model;
* borrower and loan data fields;
* repayment schedule data;
* payment status synchronisation;
* event triggers;
* webhook logic;
* CSV / JSON export requirements;
* testing environment;
* documentation format;
* delivery timeline;
* acceptance criteria;
* support period;
* and the distinction between existing platform capabilities and any custom development required specifically for ESTO.

A dedicated testing environment can be provisioned through VM Cloud as part of the integration workstream, using anonymised or synthetic data where appropriate.

Existing platform transfer, documentation, standard data exports, reasonable integration support and reasonable transition support can be included in the EUR 700,000 full operating package. Any material custom development beyond the existing QuickFund environment should be scoped separately and reflected in the final commercial terms.

Source code, software and technical assets

QuickFund is prepared to include source code transfer in the full operating package, subject to final documentation and completion.

Pre-completion technical review can be organised under controlled conditions after agreement on the commercial framework and LOI. Full source code delivery would occur at completion, together with the agreed technical documentation, deployment materials, database schema and migration support.

The transaction documentation should clearly define:

* ownership or licence treatment of the software;
* scope of source code transfer;
* treatment of third-party dependencies;
* permitted use of the software after completion;
* support obligations;
* warranty perimeter;
* and any restrictions linked to the QuickFund brand, public website or borrower portal.

QuickFund brand, domains and borrower interface

We understand ESTO’s objective of avoiding disruption for existing borrowers.

QuickFund is prepared to discuss a structure where existing borrowers continue to access their repayment information, contracts, messages and payment instructions through a stable interface during an agreed transition or run-off period.

Following completion, QuickFund would no longer be marketed as a separate active origination platform, unless expressly agreed otherwise. The public-facing website could be redirected, repositioned or limited to borrower continuity, while the borrower login area remains available for existing customers during the agreed transition or run-off period.

The treatment of the QuickFund brand, domains and public interface should be documented carefully.

Our current position is that any use of the QuickFund brand after completion should be limited to borrower continuity and servicing of the existing portfolio during the agreed run-off period. No new origination under the QuickFund brand would be permitted after completion, unless expressly agreed between the parties.

Depending on the final structure, domains and technical access can either be transferred, licensed, delegated or configured to support the agreed borrower continuity model.

Operational continuity

We agree with ESTO’s preferred model of avoiding disruption for existing borrowers.

QuickFund would be prepared to support a transition model where:

* existing borrowers continue to access their repayment information through a stable interface;
* repayment schedules remain visible;
* payment instructions are migrated in a controlled manner;
* borrower communications are coordinated;
* ESTO gradually assumes operational control;
* QuickFund’s public-facing origination activity is discontinued after completion;
* the borrower portal remains available during the agreed run-off period;
* and payment flows are migrated without unnecessary interruption.

This model should protect repayment performance and reduce operational migration risk.

Exclusivity and pre-closing technical work

We are open to discussing exclusivity as part of a non-binding letter of intent, provided that the LOI includes a clear transaction perimeter, valuation basis, legal structure, integration perimeter, transition support scope, confirmatory due diligence scope, key conditions and timetable.

If ESTO requires exclusivity and/or significant pre-closing technical work, including API or integration development before completion, QuickFund would expect an upfront transaction or integration deposit, creditable against the purchase price.

This would allow both parties to align resources, avoid an open-ended process and ensure that technical work is undertaken within a serious transaction framework.

Transaction structure

We agree that the legal form should not be the main value driver by itself. The key value drivers are enforceability, collectability, borrower continuity, compliance comfort, data migration, payment flow migration, operational control and integration practicality.

We therefore support the proposed analysis of:

* asset transaction;
* full operating asset transfer;
* corporate transaction / share acquisition;
* transitional servicing arrangement;
* staged migration subject to legal, regulatory, payment provider and data protection conditions.

If a share acquisition materially simplifies continuity or reduces transfer complexity, we are open to reviewing it. However, if such structure creates additional legal, regulatory, tax, advisory or post-closing complexity for QuickFund, this would need to be reflected in the commercial terms and documentation.

Proposed next steps

We are available to proceed with the next phase and organise the two calls suggested.

For the management call, we suggest focusing on:

* transaction structure;
* asset deal vs share acquisition;
* full operating package perimeter;
* EUR 700,000 valuation basis;
* QuickFund public website and borrower portal treatment;
* no-new-origination / run-off model;
* LOI content;
* exclusivity;
* payment mechanics;
* integration deposit if pre-closing work is required;
* confirmatory due diligence scope;
* and expected transaction timetable.

For the technical call, we suggest focusing on:

* borrower portal;
* administration panel;
* database structure;
* source code and deployment structure;
* payment and reconciliation logic;
* Stripe and bank transfer flows;
* VM Cloud hosting environment;
* webhook logic;
* CSV / JSON exports;
* API integration expectations;
* testing environment;
* migration roadmap;
* acceptance criteria;
* support and handover process.

We are also open to a joint legal counsel discussion or short legal memo comparing an asset transaction and a share acquisition from the perspective of continuity, regulatory treatment, data protection, borrower notification and execution complexity.

Please send us your proposed availability for the management call this week or early next week. We will coordinate internally to ensure that the relevant business, technical and operational contacts are available for the follow-up technical session.

Kind regards,

Gaylor Loche
CEO
QuickFund

